{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "126",
    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old Hispanic man presented with painful left lower-extremity swelling and erythema. Six months earlier, incidentally noted bilateral adrenal and periaortic retroperitoneal masses were not further evaluated. He reported intermittent fevers, episodic night sweats, decreased appetite, and palpitations. He denied trauma or injury to the leg. On examination, the left calf had ill-defined, warm, confluent erythema, edema, and tender induration. Laboratory studies showed mild lymphocytopenia, moderate anemia, elevated creatine kinase, elevated aldolase, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase; peripheral-blood flow cytometry was unremarkable. CT and MRI of the leg revealed gastrocnemius myositis, extensive subcutaneous and superficial myofascial edema, and popliteal lymphadenopathy without evident lymphatic obstruction. He was started on intravenous ceftriaxone for presumed cellulitis and pyomyositis but did not improve. A punch biopsy of skin from the lower leg showed a sparse perivascular and interstitial infiltrate of enlarged atypical mononuclear cells in the reticular dermis and subcutis, not consistent with cellulitis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Extranodal natural killer/T-cell lymphoma nasal type\nB. Cellulitis was initially favored because of the patient’s “acute swelling, erythema, warmth, and tenderness of one\nC. Pyomyositis was considered given the imaging findings and lack of antibiotic response\nD. Leukemia was contemplated in the setting of retroperitoneal masses and cytopenias but excluded when “peripheral blood\nE. Bacterial, mycobacterial, and fungal infection were ruled out as “bacterial, mycobacterial, and fungal examinations\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
